e because we  kind of revised the process  and then instead of um going back to the  executives and saying okay give us the  approval we said  well let's just have a little smaller  approval team that has the authority to  approve it  and see what they think of our our  application  so it's in their hands right now we're  trying to  we want a quick turnaround we don't want  this team to be  taking six months to give us an answer  so we're hoping to have  an answer in uh well about a month so  we'll see what happens  so what have i learned in this process  uh well one of them is just be patient  you know keep trying uh i think as we've  gone on one of the  one of the folks in the it department  where  you know five years ago he was sort of  pushed back with us he's now completely  on board and very supportive  and so i think you just have to you kind  of plant a lot of seeds  and uh you know nurture them and and  you'll get there  so i'm i'm hopeful uh and i just want to  finish with a plug or  you know an advertisement that several  years ago i started an interagency r  users group  uh and we're just kind of  informal uh and if you're interested in  uh you know are used in the government  then just  send me an email and i could add you uh  to our list  great it definitely seems like a bumpy  road and that's something i've seen at  many many federal agencies too  and some move at different paces depends  you know what their mission is and who's  there so  thank you for fighting the good fight  for r especially someone that used to be  a matlab user  and coming over to the open source it's  really amazing to hear
 

